    "title": "Bruno Fernandes transfer news: Manchester United captain not for sale at any price despite Galatasaray interest",
    "url": "https://www.skysports.com/football/news/12040/13574095/bruno-fernandes-transfer-news-manchester-united-captain-not-for-sale-at-any-price-despite-galatasaray-interest",
    "text": "<div><div>\n              \n                <p>Manchester United captain Bruno Fernandes is not for sale at any price despite interest from Galatasaray.&nbsp;</p><p>Reports have claimed the Turkish side are ready to bid for the 31-year-old, who has entered the final year of his contract, but United also have an option to extend by a further year.</p>\n<p>However, United will not entertain any offers for their talisman, who was named Premier League Player of the Year last season after breaking the competition's assist record.</p>\n<ul><li>Transfer Centre LIVE!<strong> | </strong>Man Utd news &amp; transfers🔴</li><li>Man Utd fixtures &amp; scores<strong> | </strong>FREE Man Utd highlights▶️</li><li><strong>Got Sky? </strong>Watch Premier League games LIVE on your phone📱</li><li><strong>Not got Sky? </strong>Latest Sky Sports deals<strong> or </strong>stream PL games with no contract📺</li></ul><p>The £57m release clause for foreign clubs in his contract has now expired.</p><p>In December, Fernandes revealed he had been \"hurt\" when United told him last summer he was free to accept a big-money offer to join Saudi Arabian club Al Hilal, who were ready to pay £100m as a transfer fee.</p>\n\n    \n\n<p>Despite Fernandes feeling as though the club were open to selling him last summer, the message from the hierarchy since has been clear: he is not for sale.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>The ambiguity of last summer has been put to bed with United viewing Fernandes as crucial to the future of their project.</p><div>\n    <h3>Also See:</h3>\n\n    <ul>\n        <li>\n          \n            \n              Man Utd news and transfer latest\n            \n          \n        </li>\n        <li>\n          \n            \n              Stream the Premier League with no contract\n            \n          \n        </li>\n    </ul>\n\n  </div>\n<h3>Could Man Utd really sell Fernandes?</h3>    <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>Watch footage of Manchester United captain Bruno Fernandes returning to training ahead of a new Premier League season! X credit: @ManUtd.</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p><em>Sky Sports' James Savundra on The Transfer Show:</em></p><p>\"It doesn't seem likely in this window but go back 12 months, it felt 50:50. It felt like Manchester United would be willing to sell him if they could recoup £100m to spend on other areas.</p><p>\"He spoke quite openly about the fact he spoke to the Saudi Pro League and the projects there - before deciding he wanted to commit his future to Manchester United.</p><p>\"You would think the club would be working on a contract extension because he has one year left - plus that option to extend by a further year, which you'd expect them to do so, otherwise he'd be in a position in January where he could speak to foreign clubs over a free transfer.</p><p>\"Last season he was just incredible with the assist record - he was the best player in the Premier League last season. He's a massive part of Michael Carrick's plans this season.</p><p>\"There will come a time when Manchester United think about life after Bruno Fernandes but that won't be this summer. He's so important to their plans in Europe and the Premier League.\"</p><h3>Could Zirkzee act as Fernandes cover this season?</h3> <div>\n  <figure>\n    <p><img>\n    </p>\n      <figcaption>\n        <span>Image:</span>\n        <span>Joshua Zirkzee has impressed for Manchester United in pre-season\n        </span>\n      </figcaption>\n  </figure>\n</div>\n<p><em>Sky Sports' News' Danyal Khan:</em></p><p>\"The good news for Michael Carrick and United is that two players, in particular, have stood out in pre-season.</p><p>\"Having perhaps looked a bit tired towards the end of last season, Carrick will be purring to see<strong> Bryan Mbeumo</strong> and <strong>Amad Diallo</strong> back to their electric selves in pre-season.</p><p>\"Mbeumo has looked sharp in front of goal with Amad also appearing full of confidence after an impressive World Cup with Cote d'Ivoire in the summer.</p><p>\"One player who may have a new chapter at the club is <strong>Joshua Zirkzee</strong>. Although there is still a possibility his future could be away from Old Trafford, he's looked like a different Zirkzee to the one we've seen in previous seasons.</p><p>\"Albeit it is pre-season, could Zirkzee provide cover for <strong>Fernandes </strong>in the No 10 position?</p><p>\"He was particularly impressive in Dublin against Leeds at a sold-out Croke Park, and in his third season at the club it will be intriguing to see how Carrick uses the Dutchman - if his future is at United.\"</p><ul></ul>     <p>\n            \n                \n                    \n                    \n                    \n                \n            \n\n        \n                Play Super 6 to win £1m!\n                Play Super 6 for a chance to win £1m! Enter for free.\n        \n            \n    </p>\n\n            </div></div>",
    "date": "2026-08-17T17:19:47.451Z"
  },
  {
    "id": "spo_rss_skysportsf1_aHR0cHM6Ly93d3cu",
    "source": "Sky Sports F1",
    "category": "Sports",
    "title": "F1 title race: Assessing rivals to Kimi Antonelli and factors that will decide 2026 fight ahead of Dutch GP season resumption",
    "url": "https://www.skysports.com/f1/news/12433/13572982/f1-title-race-assessing-rivals-to-kimi-antonelli-and-factors-that-will-decide-2026-fight-ahead-of-dutch-gp-season-resumption",
    "text": "<div><div>\n              \n                <p>Kimi Antonelli is the clear front-runner in Formula 1's 2026 title battle, but which drivers retain a realistic chance of catching him, and what will decide whether a comeback can be successful?</p><p>Mercedes driver Antonelli, who does not turn 20 until next week, exceeded all expectations to establish a 50-point lead at the top of the standings going into the summer break after the first 11 rounds.</p>\n<p>The season resumes live on <em>Sky Sports F1</em> this weekend with the Dutch Grand Prix, the first of 12 remaining scheduled races, and one of two remaining Sprint events.</p>\n<ul><li>Where would Kimi Antonelli rank among surprise F1 title winners?</li><li><strong>Sky Sports' Dutch GP schedule | F1 2026 standings | F1 2026 calendar</strong></li><li>Download the Sky Sports app for expert analysis, best video &amp; more<strong>📱</strong></li><li><strong>Not got Sky? </strong>Get Sky Sports<strong> or </strong>stream with no contract on NOW<strong>📺</strong></li></ul><h3>Who could catch Antonelli?</h3><p><strong>Lewis Hamilton, Ferrari - 2nd, 50 points behind Antonelli</strong></p><p>The seven-time world champion is Antonelli's nearest challenger and is driving a Ferrari that has been the car most consistently capable of challenging Mercedes during the first half of the season.</p>\n\n    \n\n    <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>On the F1 Show podcast, David Croft questioned whether Ferrari's Lewis Hamilton can challenge Antonelli for this season's world championship</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p>A scrappy run of performances before the break saw the momentum Hamilton had built earlier in the summer fizzle out, but the 41-year-old will no doubt return refreshed and fully intent on rediscovering his best form in Zandvoort.</p>\n\n\n\n<p>A strong start in Zandvoort is important for all the drivers chasing Antonelli, but perhaps especially for Hamilton and his Ferrari team-mate Charles Leclerc, who may well need to gain a significant upper hand within the team to have the support required to mount a sustained challenge.</p><div>\n    <h3>Also See:</h3>\n\n    <ul>\n        <li>\n          \n            \n              Stream every F1 race with NOW\n            \n          \n        </li>\n        <li>\n          \n            \n              Dutch GP schedule and how to watch\n            \n          \n        </li>\n        <li>\n          \n            \n              F1 team-mate head-to-head scores\n            \n          \n        </li>\n        <li>\n          \n            \n              Get Sky Sports on WhatsApp\n            \n          \n        </li>\n    </ul>\n\n  </div>\n <div>\n  <h2>Drivers' Championship - Top Five</h2>\n  <div>\n    <table>\n      <caption></caption>\n      <thead>\n      </thead>\n      <tbody>\n          <tr>\n            <td><b>Driver</b></td>\n            <td><b>Points</b></td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td>1) Kimi Antonelli</td>\n            <td>219</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td>2) Lewis Hamilton</td>\n            <td>169</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td>3) George Russell</td>\n            <td>160</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td>4) Charles Leclerc</td>\n            <td>138</td>\n          </tr>\n          <tr>\n            <td>5) Lando Norris</td>\n            <td>128</td>\n          </tr>\n      </tbody>\n    </table>\n  </div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>George Russell, Mercedes - 3rd, 59 points behind Antonelli</strong></p><p>If there was a driver who needed the summer break, it was Russell.</p><p>Luck, and then form, deserted the Brit over the first half of the season, but he still has what was undoubtedly the fastest car on the grid across the first 11 races.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>George Russell and Antonelli react to their respective seventh and third-place finishes at the Hungarian Grand Prix</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p>If a refreshed Russell, who had something to smile about over the summer break as he got engaged, can somehow rediscover his best form, he still has plenty of time to re-establish himself as a factor in the title battle.</p><p><strong>Charles Leclerc, Ferrari - 4th, 81 points behind Antonelli</strong></p><p>After a tough run of races, Leclerc appeared to finally get to grips with the SF-26 over the final month before the break.</p><p>The Monegasque won at Silverstone and outscored Hamilton at the last three races.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>Charles Leclerc won the British Grand Prix after the Safety Car was redeployed at the last moment for the final lap</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p>However, his spring struggles have left next to no margin for error during the run-in, with the challenge of hauling in Hamilton a first hurdle for him to worry about before catching Antonelli.</p><p><strong>Lando Norris, McLaren - 5th, 91 points behind Antonelli</strong></p><p>The reigning world champion was very much out of this conversation until he produced an absolutely dominant victory at the Hungarian Grand Prix in the final race before the break.</p><p>The significant upgrade package McLaren brought to Hungary appeared to work very well, and Norris may well have won by half a minute if it were not for the fact he spent half the race stuck behind his team-mate Oscar Piastri after being overtaken on the first lap.</p><p>If McLaren's car works as well in Zandvoort as it did in Budapest, Norris cannot be discounted.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>Lando Norris reacts to his first win of 2026 saying he always believed he could win and calling it one of his best personal performances</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<h3>Which factors will be decisive?</h3><p><strong>Upgrades -</strong> With this being the first season of a new regulations cycle, there remains the possibility of major swings in performance as the teams continue to bring upgrades.</p><p>Although there are minor changes to the regulations for 2027, the teams can be expected to keep pushing hard in terms of development, as most of what they bring to their cars now is likely to provide useful knowledge - and potentially performance - next season.</p><p>After seeing McLaren leap forward in Hungary, Ferrari boss Fred Vasseur pointed out that his team's approach has been to bring regular upgrades rather than big packages like the one the Woking squad had in Hungary.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>After Norris' victory last time out in Hungary, David Croft debates whether McLaren are now capable of winning further races or not</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p>Although some of the summer break represents a shutdown where work is not allowed to be done, major upgrades are expected to arrive up and down the pit lane in Zandvoort and for the Italian Grand Prix a week later.</p><p>There is also the matter of engine upgrades. The FIA's Additional Development Upgrade Opportunities (ADUO) system has already allowed Ferrari to bring an update to their power unit.</p><p>Another one could bring them closer to the performance of the field-leading Mercedes power unit, which it remains unclear whether they have been able to upgrade after it was initially, highly controversially, adjudged to be less strong than Red Bull's.</p><p><strong>Reliability </strong>- While the Mercedes power unit has been the strongest, it has also had far more major issues than Ferrari's.</p><p>Both Mercedes and McLaren, who use their rival's power unit as a customer, have suffered multiple costly failures.</p><p>Despite having less grunt, Ferrari's power unit has proved highly reliable, which has been a key factor in keeping both the team and its drivers in the hunt for this year's titles.</p><p>Antonelli, who has suffered this season at the hands of a wheel-guard failure as well as an engine blowout, will be left vulnerable if he suffers any more DNFs.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>Antonelli suffered damage during the British Grand Prix</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p><strong>Penalties - </strong>The cost of poor reliability is not necessarily limited to the weekend it occurs on, with an irreparable power unit potentially leading to penalties later on in the season.</p><p>Each driver has a limit of four power units for the season, and both Antonelli and Russell are expected to need to exceed their allowance after losing an engine to reliability earlier in the season.</p><p>Top teams usually opt to take the penalty at a circuit where overtaking is relatively easy, allowing them to use the fresh power unit to surge back through the field and minimise the potential damage.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>Russell reacts to stopping on track during qualifying at the Hungarian Grand Prix due to a water leak</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p><strong>Driving (styles) - </strong>The radical change from last year's cars to this year's has required significant adjustment from the drivers.</p><p>Russell and Leclerc comfortably outperformed their team-mates last year but have been on the receiving end this season, while Piastri has recently appeared unable to match Norris.</p><p>While Leclerc seemed to make some positive adjustments, Russell was still trying to find a method that works in Hungary, and only finding a style that works for the Brit is going to give him a chance.</p><p>As for Antonelli, Hamilton and Norris, they all seem to be enjoying the way the nimbler 2026 cars handle.</p><p>It could be fascinating to watch three generations of drivers battle at the top of their games over the remaining races.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>Highlights from the 2026 Hungarian Grand Prix</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p><strong>Experience - </strong>While it will not help in terms of pure speed, experience could be a crucial factor coming down the stretch.</p><p>Four-time world champion Max Verstappen was able to apply huge pressure to Norris and Piastri, who were both seeking a maiden title, at the end of last year.</p><p>As Antonelli potentially gets closer to the finish line, the pressure on the Italian will ramp up.</p><p>It also remains to be seen whether Hamilton, whose last title came in 2020, could feel a similarly extreme level of pressure as he seeks a record eighth championship.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>Hamilton explains why Ferrari are performing better this year compared to 2025</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p>One would expect Hamilton to be comfortable in the heat of a title battle, while Norris also holds an edge over Russell and Leclerc having come through the fire 12 months ago.</p><p>While Antonelli does not have the experience himself, his team certainly does. His race engineer Peter 'Bono' Bonnington was in Hamilton's ear for six of the Brit's seven championships, and has already played a crucial role in guiding his young driver throughout the season so far.</p><p><strong>Calendar - </strong>This element is very much out of the hands of the teams and drivers.</p><p>Ongoing conflict in the Middle East means doubt remains over whether the final two rounds of the season can go ahead as scheduled in Qatar and Abu Dhabi.</p><p>The Bahrain Grand Prix, which was unable to take place in April, is going to be held in Malaysia in October, while the Saudi Arabian Grand Prix has been lost completely.</p>     <div>\n        <div>\n            <p>Please use Chrome browser for a more accessible video player</p>\n          \n          <video></video>\n        </div>\n    \n        <figcaption>\n          \n          <span>Formula 1 has added Malaysia to its 2026 schedule to host the Bahrain Grand Prix that was postponed earlier in the season</span>\n        </figcaption>\n      </div>\n<p>F1 president Stefano Domenicali says contingency plans are in place for the last two races to be held at alternative venues in Europe, with a deadline for a decision coming around the middle of September.</p><p>However, gossip in the paddock potentially points towards only one of the two season-ending races being replaced, with Italy's Imola circuit expected to step in.</p><p>All will be revealed on the front over the next month, but one less race would, at this stage, provide a major boost for Antonelli out front, and a blow to those chasing him.</p><p><strong>The Formula 1 season resumes after the summer break with a Sprint weekend at the Dutch Grand Prix from Friday, with every session live on Sky Sports. Stream Sky Sports with NOW - no contract, cancel anytime</strong></p>\n            </div></div>",
    "date": "2026-08-17T17:19:51.262Z"
  },
