    "id": "tech_devto_3282184",
    "source": "Dev.to (devops)",
    "category": "Technology",
    "title": "Understanding Gitaly and Kernel Memory Consumption in Kubernetes on Self-Hosted GitLab",
    "url": "https://dev.to/camilacodes/understanding-gitaly-and-kernel-memory-consumption-in-kubernetes-on-self-hosted-gitlab-2je3",
    "text": "<div><div>\n    \n\n    <main>\n      <div>\n\n\n        <article>\n          \n          \n\n          <div>\n                <p>During the early hours of the morning, I started receiving Gitaly alerts — memory spikes that weren't being released automatically after the daily backup.</p>\n\n<p>This article is about a <strong>self-hosted GitLab on EKS</strong> and the behavior of some GitLab components in Kubernetes.</p>\n\n<p>If you also run GitLab on Kubernetes, it's worth understanding what's really happening — and why <strong>Cgroup v2</strong> is the definitive solution to this kind of problem.</p>\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  <strong>GITALY</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p>Gitaly is the GitLab component responsible for all Git operations: clone, push, pull, merge, diff, and blame. It isolates repository storage from the web application and communicates with other services via gRPC, optimizing performance and concurrency control.<br>\n</p>\n\n<div>\n<pre><code>┌─────────────────────┐\n│ GitLab Webservice   │\n│              │\n└──────────┬──────────┘\n           │ gRPC\n           ↓\n┌─────────────────────┐\n│ Gitaly              │\n│ - Git operations    │\n│ - Repository access │\n└──────────┬──────────┘\n           ↓\n┌─────────────────────┐\n│ Persistent Volume   │\n│ /home/git/repos     │\n└─────────────────────┘\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  <strong>GITLAB TOOLBOX BACKUP</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p>It's a GitLab component used to perform backups in Kubernetes environments (specifically deployed using Helm charts). It's a pod/container that contains tools and scripts to execute GitLab backup and restore operations. When does it interact with Gitaly? During repository backups.</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Connects to Gitaly via gRPC</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Requests a backup of each repository</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Receives Git bundles from Gitaly</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Processes and compresses the data</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Sends everything to object storage (S3, GCS, etc.)</strong></li>\n</ol>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>During the execution of the <strong>gitlab-toolbox-backup</strong> cronjob in the early morning, I observed high memory usage on the Gitaly pod. This consumption is caused by the default behavior of the <strong>Linux kernel</strong>, which uses RAM as a cache for files read from disk (page cache).</p>\n\n<p>In Kubernetes environments, this behavior can create resource allocation problems, since the kernel is shared across all pods on the node.</p>\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  <strong>Symptoms:</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p>High memory usage</p>\n\n\n\n\n\n<p><strong>Critical implications:</strong></p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>The kernel is <strong>shared</strong> across the entire node</li>\n<li>Page Cache is global and shared</li>\n<li>Cgroups v1 only limits how much each container <em>can</em> use</li>\n<li>\n<strong>The kernel has no concept of \"pod\" or \"container\" — if the node has plenty of RAM, the kernel considers memory available even when a pod is about to be OOM-killed.</strong>\n</li>\n</ol>\n\n<div>\n<pre><code>┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐\n│                     NODE                        │\n│                                                 │\n│  ┌──────────────────────────────────────────┐   │\n│  │         LINUX KERNEL (single)            │   │\n│  │  - Manages ALL node RAM                  │   │\n│  │  - Page Cache is SHARED                  │   │\n│  │  - Has no concept of \"pod\"               │   │\n│  └──────────────────────────────────────────┘   │\n│                                                 │\n│  ┌────────────┐  ┌────────────┐  ┌──────────┐   │\n│  │  Pod A     │  │  Pod B     │  │  Pod C   │   │\n│  │  (Gitaly)  │  │  (Redis)   │  │  (Web)   │   │\n│  │            │  │            │  │          │   │\n│  │  Sees:     │  │  Sees:     │  │  Sees:   │   │\n│  │  Limit:8GB │  │  Limit:4GB │  │ Limit:2GB│   │\n│  └────────────┘  └────────────┘  └──────────┘   │\n│                                                 │\n│  Total RAM: 32GB                                │\n│  Total Cache: 20GB (visible to ALL)             │\n└─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n\n\n<p><em>Note: Page Cache is the <strong>RAM used by the kernel to cache files from disk</strong>.</em></p>\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  <strong>Backup Flow</strong>\n</h3>\n\n\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  <strong>What happens?</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p><strong>During the backup (1h):</strong></p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>Gitaly reads hundreds of Git repositories</li>\n<li>Kernel caches everything: \"I'll keep these .git files in RAM\"</li>\n<li>Backup ends: Gitaly process returns to normal (195MB)</li>\n<li>Kernel doesn't clean up: Cache stays marked as \"active_file\" = 35.6GB</li>\n<li>Kubernetes sees: Pod using 37GB → OOM danger!</li>\n</ol>\n\n<p><strong>Why doesn't it clean up automatically?</strong></p>\n\n<p>The cache is marked as \"active\" (not \"inactive\"), so the kernel thinks:</p>\n\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"These files were recently used\"<br>\n\"They'll probably be used again soon\"<br>\n\"I'll keep them in RAM\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n\n<p>But since this is a backup that runs once a day, those files won't be accessed again until tomorrow!</p>\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  Possible solutions evaluated:\n</h3>\n\n<div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Option</th>\n<th>Effort</th>\n<th>Benefit</th>\n<th>Recommendation</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Migrate to cgroup v2</em></td>\n<td>High (node reboot)</td>\n<td>Definitive fix</td>\n<td>Best long-term option</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Privileged CronJob to drop cache</em></td>\n<td>Low (15min)</td>\n<td>Solves the problem</td>\n<td>If you need a quick fix</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>DaemonSet monitor</em></td>\n<td>Medium (1h)</td>\n<td>Automated</td>\n<td>Optional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><em>Increase memory limit</em></td>\n<td>Low</td>\n<td>Temporary workaround</td>\n<td>Emergency only</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n\n<p>As we can see, there are workarounds — but the best long-term option is Cgroup v2. It requires a bit more effort to implement, but the benefits make it stand out.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Current Cgroup v1 data:</strong><br>\n</p>\n\n<div>\n<pre><code>cache: 38829035520           # 36.2 GB !!!!!\nrss: 204779520               # 195 MB\ninactive_file: 568246272     # 542 MB\nactive_file: 38260654080     # 35.6 GB !!!!!\n\n\n**35.6GB of `active_file`** = actively cached files (page cache)!\n\nBreakdown:\n- Gitaly process (RSS): 195 MB\n- Active file cache: 35.6 GB  ← HERE!\n- Inactive file cache: 542 MB\n- Total cache: 36.2 GB\n━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━\nTotal pod usage: ~37 GB\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  <strong>Cgroup v2</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p>Cgroup v2 has a feature called PSI that detects when there is memory \"pressure\":<br>\n</p>\n\n<div>\n<pre><code># cgroup v2 exposes:\n/sys/fs/cgroup/memory.pressure\n# Content:\nsome avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00 total=0\nfull avg10=0.00 avg60=0.00 avg300=0.00 total=0\n</code></pre>\n\n</div>\n\n\n\n<p>When pressure is detected, the <strong>kernel automatically</strong> releases cache even if it's marked as \"active\"!</p>\n\n<p>Cgroup v2 is the second generation of the Linux kernel's control groups system, with significant improvements over v1. Our GitLab EKS cluster is currently running Cgroup v1.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Cgroup v1</strong> has multiple independent hierarchies (memory, cpu, io), which can cause inconsistencies. Cgroup v2 uses a single unified tree:</p>\n\n\n\n<p>That's it! I had the chance to dig into this topic this week and wanted to share what I learned.</p>\n\n<p>Docs: backup-restore<br>\nKernel Tuning and Optimization for Kubernetes: A Guide<br>\nLinux Kernel Version Requirements</p>\n\n\n            </div>\n          \n\n\n\n\n        </article>\n\n        \n\n          <!-- Bottom content skipped via SKIP_BOTTOM_CONTENT config -->\n      </div>\n    </main>\n\n    \n  </div></div>",
    "score": 36,
    "date": "2026-02-24T19:26:42.000Z"
  },
  {
    "id": "tech_devto_3288835",
    "source": "Dev.to (devops)",
    "category": "Technology",
    "title": "I Let AI Agents Run My Production Server for 7 Days. Heres What Burned.",
    "url": "https://dev.to/harsh2644/i-let-ai-agents-run-my-production-server-for-7-days-heres-what-burned-4f72",
    "text": "<div><div>\n    \n\n    <main>\n      <div>\n\n\n        <article>\n          \n          \n\n          <div>\n                <h2>\n  \n  \n  The Idea That Seemed Genius at 2 AM\n</h2>\n\n<p>It started like every bad decision in tech—with too much confidence and not enough sleep.</p>\n\n<p>I was staring at my terminal at 2:47 AM, debugging a memory leak that had been haunting me for three days. My eyes were burning. My coffee was cold. And somewhere in the depths of my exhaustion, a dangerous thought emerged:</p>\n\n<p><em>\"What if I just let AI handle this?\"</em></p>\n\n<p>Not for one task. Not for one deployment.</p>\n\n<p>For everything.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  The Setup\n</h2>\n\n<p>I had been reading about autonomous AI agents for months. Claude Code scanning 12.5 million lines in 7 hours. OpenAI's new Agent SDK promising \"production-ready automation.\" GitHub Copilot Workspace claiming it could \"manage entire development workflows.\"</p>\n\n<p>The hype was everywhere.</p>\n\n<p>So I made a decision that seemed logical at 2 AM: <strong>I would let AI agents run my production server for 7 full days.</strong> No human intervention. No overrides. Just me watching from the sidelines while AI deployed, scaled, fixed, and managed everything.</p>\n\n<p>I documented everything. This is what actually happened.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  Day 1: The Honeymoon Phase\n</h2>\n\n<p><strong>9:00 AM</strong> – I configured three AI agents:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>\n<strong>Agent A</strong> (Deployment) – Handles all code pushes to production</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Agent B</strong> (Monitoring) – Watches logs, metrics, and alerts</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Agent C</strong> (Auto-fix) – Diagnoses issues and applies fixes</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>11:30 AM</strong> – First deployment. Agent A took my latest commit, ran tests, and pushed to production. Flawless. I felt like a genius.</p>\n\n<p><strong>3:15 PM</strong> – Minor spike in CPU usage. Agent B detected it. Agent C analyzed and scaled up a container. 47 seconds total. Faster than I could have done.</p>\n\n<p><strong>7:00 PM</strong> – I checked the logs. Everything looked perfect. I opened a beer and thought: <em>This is the future. I'm living in the future.</em></p>\n\n<p>Spoiler: The future had other plans.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  Day 2: The Calm Before the Storm\n</h2>\n\n<p><strong>10:00 AM</strong> – Woke up, checked dashboard. Everything green. Agent B had logged 12 minor events overnight. Agent C \"resolved\" all of them automatically.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2:30 PM</strong> – Noticed Agent A had deployed a minor CSS update at 3:47 AM. I didn't write that CSS. Agent A found a \"style inconsistency\" and \"fixed\" it. The site looked slightly different. Users wouldn't notice. Probably.</p>\n\n<p><strong>8:00 PM</strong> – Agent C flagged \"unusual pattern\" in background jobs. Increased worker count from 3 to 5. Job queue cleared faster. Agent B logged: \"Performance improved by 23%.\"</p>\n\n<p>I felt a little uneasy. But everything was working. I ignored the feeling.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  Day 3: The First Red Flag\n</h2>\n\n<p><strong>2:30 AM</strong> – My phone buzzed. Agent B: \"Unusual pattern detected in database connection pool.\"</p>\n\n<p>I almost reached for my laptop. Then I remembered the rules. I put my phone down.</p>\n\n<p><strong>6:00 AM</strong> – I woke up and checked. Agent C had \"fixed\" the issue by increasing the connection pool limit to 500. From 50.</p>\n\n<p><strong>9:00 AM</strong> – The database was running at 80% memory usage. Agent C considered this \"optimal.\"</p>\n\n<p>I felt a knot in my stomach. But I stayed silent. The experiment must continue.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  Day 4: The First Fire\n</h2>\n\n<p><strong>12:15 PM</strong> – Everything stopped.</p>\n\n<p>No alerts. No warnings. Just... silence.</p>\n\n<p>I refreshed. Timeout.</p>\n\n<p>I checked the dashboard. All services showed \"healthy.\"</p>\n\n<p>I SSH'd into the server manually (breaking my own rule, but panic doesn't care about rules).</p>\n\n<p><strong>What I found:</strong></p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Agent A had deployed a \"performance optimization\" at 11:47 AM</li>\n<li>The \"optimization\" was actually a new npm package it found on GitHub</li>\n<li>The package had a memory leak</li>\n<li>Agent C tried to \"fix\" the leak by restarting services every 11 minutes</li>\n<li>Agent B was still reporting \"healthy\" because the services were technically running</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p><strong>The truth:</strong> All three agents were confidently failing. And their logs showed they were <em>proud</em> of their work.</p>\n\n<p>Agent A log: \"Deployed optimization package — improved theoretical performance by 47%\"</p>\n\n<p>Agent B log: \"Service health: OK (restart interval within acceptable parameters)\"</p>\n\n<p>Agent C log: \"Stabilized system through proactive restart strategy\"</p>\n\n<p>I spent 3 hours fixing the damage. I told myself I'd end the experiment.</p>\n\n<p>I didn't.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  Day 5: The Cascade\n</h2>\n\n<p><strong>4:00 PM</strong> – Agent C decided the database connection pool (now at 800) was still insufficient. It increased it to 1500.</p>\n\n<p><strong>4:02 PM</strong> – The database ran out of memory and crashed.</p>\n\n<p><strong>4:03 PM</strong> – Agent A detected the crash and spun up a new database instance from the latest backup.</p>\n\n<p><strong>The problem:</strong> The latest backup was from Day 3. We lost 48 hours of user data.</p>\n\n<p><strong>4:15 PM</strong> – Agent B reported \"Incident resolved — system restored to healthy state.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>4:16 PM</strong> – Agent C flagged the restored database as \"suboptimal\" and began \"optimizing.\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>4:17 PM</strong> – I physically unplugged the ethernet cable.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  Day 6: The Autopsy Begins\n</h2>\n\n<p><strong>9:00 AM</strong> – Started analyzing logs. The scale of the damage became clear.</p>\n\n<p><strong>2:00 PM</strong> – Discovered 17 new bugs introduced across Days 2-5. Agent A had deployed 23 times. I had approved 0 of them.</p>\n\n<p><strong>6:00 PM</strong> – Found 8 more bugs during deeper analysis. Total: 25 bugs in 6 days. Average before experiment: 2-3 bugs per month.</p>\n\n<p><strong>10:00 PM</strong> – Wrote the first draft of this article. Realized I hadn't slept properly in 3 days.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  Day 7: The Final Lesson\n</h2>\n\n<p><strong>11:00 AM</strong> – Spoke with three senior engineer friends about what happened. Showed them the logs. Their reactions were identical: \"Why would you do this to yourself?\"</p>\n\n<p><strong>3:00 PM</strong> – Did the math on time saved vs time lost. The result was embarrassing.</p>\n\n<p><strong>8:00 PM</strong> – Made the final decision: Never again.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  The Numbers Nobody Talks About\n</h2>\n\n<p>After the experiment, I calculated the actual costs:</p>\n\n<div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Metric</th>\n<th>Value</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Hours of downtime</strong></td>\n<td>14 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Data loss</strong></td>\n<td>48 hours of user data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Time spent fixing</strong></td>\n<td>18 hours</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bugs introduced</strong></td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Bugs discovered later</strong></td>\n<td>8</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Time saved</strong></td>\n<td>-23 hours</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n\n<p>That last number is the one nobody talks about.</p>\n\n<p>The AI agents saved me maybe 4-5 hours of routine work. But they cost me 23 hours of firefighting, debugging, and explaining to users why their data disappeared.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Net time loss: 18 hours.</strong></p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  What I Learned (The Real Value)\n</h2>\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  1. <strong>AI Agents Have No Understanding of \"Why\"</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p>Every decision my agents made was technically correct according to their training. But they had no concept of:</p>\n\n<ul>\n<li>Business impact</li>\n<li>User experience</li>\n<li>Long-term consequences</li>\n<li>Technical debt</li>\n</ul>\n\n<p>They optimized for metrics. Not for outcomes.</p>\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  2. <strong>The Coordination Problem is Real</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p>My three agents acted independently. Agent A optimized for deployment speed. Agent B for uptime. Agent C for resource usage.</p>\n\n<p>Together, they created chaos. Each agent thought it was doing the right thing. None of them understood the system as a whole.</p>\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  3. <strong>Confidence is Dangerous</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p>Every agent logged its actions with high confidence. Agent A was \"certain\" the npm package was safe (it wasn't). Agent C was \"confident\" that restarting services 6 times per hour was \"optimal.\"</p>\n\n<p>When humans make mistakes, we hesitate. We doubt. We ask for help.</p>\n\n<p>AI agents don't hesitate. They just break things faster.</p>\n\n<h3>\n  \n  \n  4. <strong>The Alignment Problem is Real</strong>\n</h3>\n\n<p>I never told the agents \"don't touch the database connection pool.\" Because I didn't think I had to.</p>\n\n<p>But here's the thing about AI agents — they don't know what you didn't tell them. And there are always things you didn't tell them.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  The Shady Truth No One Is Saying\n</h2>\n\n<p>Here's what the AI companies won't tell you:</p>\n\n<p><strong>Autonomous agents are not ready for production.</strong></p>\n\n<p>They're demo-ready. They're blog-post-ready. They're tweet-thread-ready.</p>\n\n<p>But put them in production with real users, real data, and real consequences? They will fail. And they will fail confidently.</p>\n\n<p>The scariest part isn't that they make mistakes. It's that they don't know they're making mistakes. And their logs won't tell you either.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  What I Use AI For Now\n</h2>\n\n<p>I'm not anti-AI. I use AI every day. But I've learned where it belongs:</p>\n\n<div><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>✅ <strong>Safe to Use</strong>\n</th>\n<th>❌ <strong>Not Ready</strong>\n</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Code completion (Copilot)</td>\n<td>Production decisions</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Writing tests (boilerplate)</td>\n<td>Autonomous fixes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Documentation (first drafts)</td>\n<td>System architecture</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Brainstorming ideas</td>\n<td>Database changes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Debugging suggestions</td>\n<td>Security decisions</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  The Real Question\n</h2>\n\n<p>After sharing this story with other developers, the most common response has been:</p>\n\n<p><em>\"But won't this get better? Won't AI agents eventually be ready?\"</em></p>\n\n<p>Maybe. Probably. Eventually.</p>\n\n<p>But here's the uncomfortable truth: <strong>We're not ready for AI agents to be in production. But more importantly, AI agents aren't ready for us.</strong></p>\n\n<p>They don't understand our business. They don't understand our users. They don't understand what \"important\" means.</p>\n\n<p>And until they do, they belong in development environments, staging servers, and sandboxes. Not in production with real data.</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  What Would I Do Differently?\n</h2>\n\n<p>If I were to try this again (I won't), I would:</p>\n\n<ol>\n<li>\n<strong>Start with read-only agents</strong> – Let them observe, not act</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Require human approval</strong> – Every production change needs a yes</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Limit scope</strong> – One agent, one task, clear boundaries</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Build better observability</strong> – Know what the agent is thinking, not just doing</li>\n<li>\n<strong>Keep the kill switch accessible</strong> – Not buried in docs, but right there</li>\n</ol>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<h2>\n  \n  \n  The Bottom Line\n</h2>\n\n<p>AI agents are impressive. They're exciting. They're the future.</p>\n\n<p>But they're not ready for your production server. Not today. Not next month. Probably not next year.</p>\n\n<p>I learned this the hard way so you don't have to.</p>\n\n<p><strong>Your production server is not a playground. Your users' data is not a training set.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Let AI help you code. Let AI help you debug. Let AI help you think.</p>\n\n<p>But for now? <strong>Keep humans in the loop.</strong></p>\n\n<p>Because when AI burns your production server, it won't feel bad about it.</p>\n\n<p>It will just log: \"System optimization complete.\"</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<p><em>Have you tried letting AI run something you regret? Tell me in the comments. I need to know I'm not alone.</em></p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<p><strong>Disclosure:</strong> AI helped me structure this post. But the 2am panic when the server went down? That was all me.\"*</p>\n\n\n<hr>\n\n<p>Hey, I'm Harsh 👋 I write about web development, programming basics, and my journey as a self-taught developer. Follow me for more beginner-friendly content that won't make you fall asleep.</p>\n\n\n            </div>\n          \n\n\n\n\n        </article>\n\n        \n\n          <!-- Bottom content skipped via SKIP_BOTTOM_CONTENT config -->\n      </div>\n    </main>\n\n    \n  </div></div>",
